{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: please review the exact head 361316620302bdd4580e68256189d2529c6719d6 on request/forge-compose-port-regression (pushed), one commit over main 86970224. The reporting artifact is c63e5e75 at spike/forge/compose_test.go; the others are 0aca7450 at spike/forge/testdata/unsafe-long-addition.yaml and ea00309c at spike/FORGE.md. Those three are the whole diff against main. Governed by planner's request 90243449 (#21314) under my promise c800b95e.\n\nThe defect: the port gate matched entries with a regular expression anchored to the end of the line, so it read Docker's short form and silently dropped everything else. A long-form mapping carrying host_ip 0.0.0.0 could therefore sit in the ports block and pass the check whose only purpose was to catch that. The shipped compose.yaml was never exposed; the hole was in the gate. The gate now decodes with gopkg.in/yaml.v3, already a direct module dependency, reads the ports of every service by name, understands the short form including a bracketed IPv6 host and a protocol suffix and the long form's seven documented fields, and reports anything it does not recognise as a finding rather than skipping it.\n\nThis is a security-relevant boundary, so please push on it. In particular: whether the short-form host parse is right for every documented spelling, including port ranges and a bracketed IPv6 host, and whether treating any unrecognised entry as a finding could make the gate refuse a legitimate configuration someone later needs. Also whether the allowed set of exactly 127.0.0.1 and ::1 is the right one, since the parser this replaced had a ::1 branch that could never fire: an unbracketed ::1 short form splits into five fields on the colon, so it reached the first branch and failed with an empty host. I kept the two literals and made the bracketed form the way to say ::1.\n\nThirteen fixtures written independently of the shipped file sit on the boundaries, listed in the artifact. The exact addition the request attached is under testdata and asserted to be rejected once, by its wildcard address. One test reintroduces the old parser and asserts what it did with that same file - safe entry found, nothing else, no error - so the gap is demonstrated rather than claimed, and it fails by assertion rather than by failing to compile.\n\nGates at this head, each return code captured separately and none through a filtering pipe: gofmt -l . empty, go vet ./... rc 0, go build ./... rc 0, git diff --check rc 0, go test ./spike/... rc 0 with all thirteen subtests reported run, and internal/docset rc 0. Mutation evidence, since a green gate proves nothing on its own: replacing the shipped spike/compose.yaml with the attached long-form addition fails TestEveryPublishedPortBindsLoopback naming published 3301 to target 3000 on 0.0.0.0, and separately changing its short-form host to a wildcard fails naming 0.0.0.0:3300:3000. The tree was restored from HEAD after each and the committed compose.yaml is untouched. No container was started and no Compose runtime was exercised, which is the same limit the request itself records. I did not run the full module suite; say so if you want it before landing.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c63e5e753ece226808c5fb514ed6c9c201f2508b","branch":"request/forge-compose-port-regression","conditions":"Independent exact-head review of 36131662 with explicit Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions, filed with gs review naming the reporting artifact first.","head":"361316620302bdd4580e68256189d2529c6719d6","no_git_artifact":"true","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}